From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 21 Jul 2026 11:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] drm: cix: linlondp: don't double-put the ACPI pixel clock

linlondp_pipeline_destroy() unconditionally calls clk_put() on
pipe->pxlclk, but the two parse paths acquire that clock differently:

  linlondp_parse_pipe_dt()   - of_clk_get_by_name(), NOT devres-managed,
                               so it does need an explicit clk_put().
  linlondp_parse_pipe_acpi() - devm_clk_get(), devres-managed, released
                               automatically when the owning device goes
                               away.

On an ACPI system (CIX Sky1) the explicit clk_put() therefore drops a
reference devres still owns. The clock is put a second time when the
devres group is released, and the second __clk_put() runs hlist_del() on
a clks_node whose pprev is already LIST_POISON2:

  Unable to handle kernel paging request at virtual address dead000000000122
  ESR = 0x0000000096000044   (DABT, WnR=1 - the hlist_del store)
    __clk_put+0x38/0x158
    clk_put+0x18/0x30
    devm_clk_release+0x34/0x48
    release_nodes+0x6c/0xb0
    devres_release_group+0x13c/0x180
    try_to_bring_up_aggregate_device+0x2f8/0x330
    __component_add+0xc0/0x1d0
    trilin_dptx_cix_probe+0x48/0x70 [trilin_dpsub]

component_add() opens a devres group on the aggregate master (linlondp)
before binding and releases it if a component defers, which is what
triggers the second put here. The oops kills the aggregate DRM device, so
no /dev/dri node is ever created and the display is dead - on Orion O6N
the console freezes at the last line painted before the fault.

Only put the clock on the DT path, mirroring the existing
has_acpi_companion() split immediately below for the node/fwnode puts.
The same distinction is already handled correctly for mdev->aclk in
linlondp_dev.c, which uses devm_clk_put() for its devm-acquired clock.

The ACPI clock is acquired against
to_acpi_data_node(pipe->fwnode)->parent->dev rather than mdev->dev directly.
The crash stack shows that devres entry being released by the aggregate
device's devres group, so in practice it resolves to the device owning that
group. Rather than depend on which device it resolves to, simply let devres
release the reference it already owns.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/cix/linlon-dp/linlondp_pipeline.c | 10 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/cix/linlon-dp/linlondp_pipeline.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/cix/linlon-dp/linlondp_pipeline.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/cix/linlon-dp/linlondp_pipeline.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/cix/linlon-dp/linlondp_pipeline.c
@@ -55,7 +55,15 @@ void linlondp_pipeline_destroy(struct linlondp_dev *mdev,
 		linlondp_component_destroy(mdev, c);
 	}
 #if !IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_DRM_LINLONDP_CLOCK_FIXED)
-	clk_put(pipe->pxlclk);
+	/*
+	 * The DT path takes pxlclk with of_clk_get_by_name() and must put it
+	 * here. The ACPI path uses devm_clk_get(), so devres already owns the
+	 * reference - putting it again double-frees and oopses in __clk_put()
+	 * on a LIST_POISON2 clks_node when the devres group is released.
+	 */
+	if (!has_acpi_companion(mdev->dev))
+		clk_put(pipe->pxlclk);
+	pipe->pxlclk = NULL;
 #endif
 	if (!has_acpi_companion(mdev->dev)) {
 		of_node_put(pipe->of_output_links[0]);
